[Handbook: erythema annulare centrifigum]
This curious, but uncommon, condition is thought to be a ‘hypersensitivity reaction’ perhaps to drugs or infection, but, in most cases, a trigger is not identified. Small dermal blood vessels are cuffed by clusters of lymphocytes. Erythema annulare centrifugum (EAC) is commoner in adults than children. Do not confuse this with the other erythemas (see Box 11.6).  This curious, but uncommon, condition is thought to be a ‘hypersensitivity reaction’ perhaps to drugs or infection, but, in most cases, a trigger is not identified. Small dermal blood vessels are cuffed by clusters of lymphocytes. Erythema annulare centrifugum (EAC) is commoner in adults than children. Do not confuse this with the other erythemas (see Box 11.6).
The greasy,ill-defined erythematous papular rash involves the central face but is not oedematous and spares the eyelids. The scalp may be erythematous and scaly, but hair is not usually affected. Seborrhoeic dermatitis may also involve the central chest, central back, and flexures.  The greasy,ill-defined erythematous papular rash involves the central face but is not oedematous and spares the eyelids. The scalp may be erythematous and scaly, but hair is not usually affected. Seborrhoeic dermatitis may also involve the central chest, central back, and flexures.

[Handbook: psoriasis]
Patients may have ${>}1$ pattern of psoriasis:  Patients may have ${>}1$ pattern of psoriasis:  · Psoriasis vulgaris (large plaque and/or small plaque) on extensor surfaces. · Psoriasis vulgaris (large plaque and/or small plaque) on extensor surfaces.
Patients with psoriasis have an increased risk of developing other inflammatory diseases that are immunologically mediated—seronegative arthritis (psoriatic arthritis——see $spadesuit$ p. 206) and Crohn disease. Case control studies have also shown an increased prevalence of the metabolic syndrome (see Box 9.2) in patients with psoriasis, possibly related to chronic inflammation and circulating inflammatory cytokines, including TNF- $upalpha$ . The psychosocial burden of psoriasis is considerable. Anxiety and depression are common, and patients tend to smoke and drink too much alcohol. Treat the whole patient, remembering associated co-​morbidities, including coeliac disease, cardiovascular disease (see ${sf B o x9.2p}$ . 191), hypertension, obesity, type 2 diabetes, peptic ulcer, and liver disease.  Patients with psoriasis have an increased risk of developing other inflammatory diseases that are immunologically mediated—seronegative arthritis (psoriatic arthritis——see $\spadesuit$ p. 206) and Crohn disease. Case control studies have also shown an increased prevalence of the metabolic syndrome (see Box 9.2) in patients with psoriasis, possibly related to chronic inflammation and circulating inflammatory cytokines, including TNF- $\upalpha$ . The psychosocial burden of psoriasis is considerable. Anxiety and depression are common, and patients tend to smoke and drink too much alcohol. Treat the whole patient, remembering associated co-​morbidities, including coeliac disease, cardiovascular disease (see ${\sf B o x9.2p}$ . 191), hypertension, obesity, type 2 diabetes, peptic ulcer, and liver disease.

[Handbook: pustular psoriasis]
Rarely, pustulosis may affect one or more digits in the absence of other forms of psoriasis (see $spadesuit$ Box 9.9 and p. 208).  Rarely, pustulosis may affect one or more digits in the absence of other forms of psoriasis (see $\spadesuit$ Box 9.9 and p. 208).
•    Chronic psoriasis: occasionally very hyperkeratotic, particularly on the palms and soles (see $spadesuit$ pp. 189–208). Reiter syndrome, which is pustular and hyperkeratotic, is probably a variant of psoriasis (see $spadesuit$ p. 208). Usually very symmetrical. •    Chronic psoriasis: occasionally very hyperkeratotic, particularly on the palms and soles (see $\spadesuit$ pp. 189–208). Reiter syndrome, which is pustular and hyperkeratotic, is probably a variant of psoriasis (see $\spadesuit$ p. 208). Usually very symmetrical.

[Handbook: erythema multiforme]
