ook entry for porphyria (specifically pseudoporphyria) provide the best match for the observed morphology and distribution. The other diagnoses (prurigo nodularis, kaposi sarcoma, epidermolysis bullosa, nevocytic nevus) do not align with the described features: prurigo nodularis typically presents with chronic nodules and plaques from chronic rubbing, kaposi sarcoma presents with vascular nodules, epidermolysis bullosa presents with intraepidermal blisters, and nevocytic nevus presents with pigmented lesions following Blaschko lines. The absence of precedent cases does not negate the diagnostic fit with porphyria, as pseudoporphyria is an acquired condition that may not have been previously documented in similar cases.'}
