nosis over the other options. Scabies typically presents with vesicopustules and intense itching, which are absent here. Neurotic excoriations lack objective physical findings. Folliculitis would show inflammatory follicles, not this pattern of pigmentation and ulceration. Lymphangioma typically presents as dome-shaped papules or macules, not this complex, ulcerative, and pigmented presentation. The absence of precedent cases does not negate the diagnosis, as naevus comedonicus is a rare condition with variable presentations. The visual findings and the known association of this condition with segmental, pigmented, and ulcerative lesions make naevus comedonicus the most likely diagnosis.'}
