Incontinentia pigmenti (IP) is a rare ectodermal dysplasia, usually lethal in $bigcirc$ , caused by mutations in ${mathsf{N F}}{mathsf{-}}{mathsf{kappa}}{mathsf{B}}$ essential modulator gene (IKBKG/ NEMO) on the $times$ chromosome. $mathcal{G}$ are mosaics (random $times$ chromosome inactivation). NEMO protein has a key role in the NF-kB signalling pathway involved in many functions, including cell survival. Loss of NEMO—​ cells are more sensitive to apoptosis.  Incontinentia pigmenti (IP) is a rare ectodermal dysplasia, usually lethal in $\bigcirc$ , caused by mutations in ${\mathsf{N F}}{\mathsf{-}}{\mathsf{\kappa}}{\mathsf{B}}$ essential modulator gene (IKBKG/ NEMO) on the $\times$ chromosome. $\mathcal{G}$ are mosaics (random $\times$ chromosome inactivation). NEMO protein has a key role in the NF-kB signalling pathway involved in many functions, including cell survival. Loss of NEMO—​ cells are more sensitive to apoptosis.
