        [1. Current Visual Findings (from Vision Agent)]:
        This is a close-up image of a solitary, well-circumscribed, erythematous papule located at the distal nail fold of the thumb. The lesion exhibits a dome-shaped, slightly raised, and firm appearance with a smooth, glistening surface. The coloration is predominantly erythematous with a central area of hyperkeratotic, roughened, and slightly yellowish-brown discoloration, suggesting chronicity or secondary hyperpigmentation. The borders are sharply demarcated from the surrounding normal skin, with no evidence of notching or irregularity. There is no visible scaling, crusting, ulceration, or telangiectasia. The lesion is asymmetrically situated and lacks any internal structural patterns such as a pigment network or globules. The adjacent nail plate appears intact and shows no signs of onycholysis or subungual involvement. The overall morphology and location are consistent with a chronic inflammatory or reactive process, possibly representing a localized granulomatous or psoriatic-like reaction at the nail fold, or a focal dystrophic change.

