t an ulcerated, exudative lesion; congenital nevus is a pigmented lesion without ulceration; neutrophilic dermatoses like pyoderma gangrenosum may present with ulceration but typically have a more rapid onset and often show a punched-out appearance with undermined borders, which is not fully described here; Stevens-Johnson syndrome is a systemic reaction with widespread mucocutaneous involvement, not a solitary scalp lesion. The model’s prediction of folliculitis (7.30%) is modest but supported by the visual evidence, which strongly suggests a bacterial etiology. No precedent cases were found, but the clinical presentation is most consistent with a severe, chronic bacterial folliculitis.'}
